"""Report part A: front matter, introduction, system description, parameter registry, process and logistics modelling."""
from ctx import *
from doclib import f0, f1, f2, pct
import registry as REG

TITLE = "Re-assessing microalgae fuel against the Philippine biodiesel benchmark"
SUB = "An integrated techno-economic and life cycle assessment of the San Carlos flue-gas design, with scenario, prospective, uncertainty, optimisation and machine-learning surrogate analyses"

def front(d):
    d.add("title", TITLE); d.add("subtitle", SUB)
    d.add("meta", "Internal technical report, version 1.1 (revised after internal review)")
    d.add("meta", [{"t": "Shulamite G. Cereno", "b": True}, {"t": "1", "sup": True}, " and ", {"t": "Ralph Carlo Evidente", "b": True}, {"t": "2", "sup": True}])
    d.add("meta", [{"t": "1", "sup": True}, " National Graduate School of Engineering, University of the Philippines Diliman, Quezon City, Philippines"])
    d.add("meta", [{"t": "2", "sup": True}, " BRIGHT (Biotechnology Research Institute for the Green Transition), Technical University of Denmark, Kgs. Lyngby, Denmark"])
    d.add("meta", "17 October 2026. All money in US dollars on a mixed 2025–2026 basis: capital in USD 2025 (CEPCI 2025 annual average, the latest complete year) and prices and operating costs at 2026 quotes (PHP 60.75 per USD); no escalation is applied between the two.")
    d.h1("Abstract", bm="sec_abs")
    d.p(f"The Philippine Biofuels Act requires all diesel to carry locally made biodiesel, which today is coconut methyl ester (CME) at about {f2(B['p_cme'])} USD per litre. The Act defines biodiesel as FAME or another mono-alkyl ester from vegetable oil or animal fats approved by the Department of Energy, so CME is used here as an economic benchmark; eligibility of HTL-derived renewable diesel, or of algal FAME, to meet the mandate is not assumed and would require a separate regulatory determination [@ra9367]. This report re-assesses a 2018 plant design and its 2021 techno-economic evaluation for producing fuel from *Chlorella vulgaris* grown on flue gas from the 18 MW San Carlos BioPower plant in Negros Occidental [@cereno2018,aviso2021]. A single process model drives both a bottom-up techno-economic assessment (TEA) and an attributional life cycle assessment (LCA) on ecoinvent 3.12, for two routes: whole-algae hydrothermal liquefaction with hydrotreating (HTL) and wet lipid extraction with transesterification (FAME).")
    d.p(f"Recomputing the inherited balance and workbooks from first principles, {NVFAIL} of {NVER} checks do not hold; each is traced to a workbook sheet and cell or a thesis page. The most consequential is that 79.5 of the 80 t/d of algae entering the plant are fed in rather than grown; growing them at 20 g/m²/d, near the upper end of measured outdoor productivity, needs about {f0(CORE['area80'])} ha of ponds to deliver 24,000 t/yr to conversion, instead of 10.4 ha. For a corrected 400 ha design the minimum fuel selling price (MFSP) is {f2(H['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD per litre of diesel-equivalent for HTL and {f2(F['mfsp_ldeq'])} for FAME, against {f2(CME)} for CME and {f2(DSL)} for diesel before tax. Life-cycle emissions are {f1(H['gwp'])} and {f1(F['gwp'])} g CO₂e/MJ against the 94 g/MJ RED II fossil comparator: the base HTL route provides negligible deterministic GHG savings ({H['saving']*100:.1f}%) and FAME {pct(F['saving'])}, because the Visayas grid supplies the electricity for mixing, CO₂ delivery and dewatering. In a hypothetical power purchase agreement with the neighbouring biomass plant at the Green Energy Auction tariff, the HTL footprint halves ({f1(S['S27']['gwp'])} g/MJ), and clean utilities reach {f1(S['S32']['gwp'])} g/MJ ({pct(S['S32']['saving'])} saving) at almost no change in cost; supply and contract availability are not established.")
    d.p(f"Sixty scenarios, one-at-a-time and global sensitivity analysis (Morris; Sobol' with {SOB['HTL']['n']:,} base samples), {NMC:,}-draw Monte Carlo simulation with rank-correlated inputs, stochastic cash flows with typhoon outages and mean-reverting prices, NSGA-II optimisation and five machine-learning surrogates were run on the same engine. With independent inputs, areal productivity and biocrude yield have first-order Sobol' indices summing to {pct(s1sum('HTL')[0])} of the variance of the HTL MFSP. In 2026 none of the evaluated scenarios, and no design in the optimisation domain, reaches the CME price; the base designs do so in no more than 1 of 10,000 Monte Carlo draws; the break-even productivity is {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL)'))} g/m²/d at 400 ha and {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL, 2,000 ha)'))} at 2,000 ha. Along a net-zero pathway with learning and biological progress the HTL MFSP falls to {f2(S["S54"]["mfsp_ldeq"])} USD/L deq in 2050; under these assumptions the model probability of an MFSP below the fixed CME benchmark is {pct(pr('S54 HTL NZ 2050','p_lt_cme'))} ({pct(pr('S54 HTL NZ 2050','p_lt_cme_sampled'))} against the sampled CME price), a conditional model result rather than a forecast. A neural-network surrogate reproduces the engine with R² of {MLM['HTL']['Neural network (MLP 64-64)']['mfsp_ldeq']['r2']:.4f} for MFSP and drives a published web calculator. Microalgae fuel from this site is not a near-term route to cheaper biodiesel-blend supply; on the modelled pathways its cost falls below the CME benchmark in {XOVER[('HTL','NZ')]} (net zero), {XOVER[('HTL','CES2')]} (CES2) and {XOVER[('HTL','CES1')]} (CES1), and not by 2050 on the Reference pathway, provided power decarbonises, productivity rises toward 30 g/m²/d and capital costs follow a learning curve.")
    d.small("Keywords: microalgae; hydrothermal liquefaction; biodiesel mandate; techno-economic assessment; life cycle assessment; Monte Carlo; Sobol' indices; NSGA-II; surrogate model; Philippines.")
    d.h2("Authorship and contribution statement")
    d.p("Shulamite G. Cereno: original plant design, material and energy balances, plant layout and financial model (2018); techno-economic and multi-attribute evaluation (2021); data curation of the original study; review of this report. Ralph Carlo Evidente: conceptualisation of the re-assessment; methodology; software; verification and validation; formal analysis; visualisation; writing of the original draft. The 2018 design was co-authored with D. M. D. Gayares and R. C. R. Suplito and the 2021 evaluation with A. L. Aviso, R. J. M. Mallorca and N. C. R. Reyes [@cereno2018,aviso2021]; their findings are reproduced in the project database and credited where used.")
    d.h2("Declaration of AI use")
    d.p("During the preparation of this work the authors used an AI agent, Claude (Anthropic), to write and run the model code, carry out the analyses, produce the figures, the project database and the prototype web calculator, check references against Crossref and publisher records, and draft the text. The authors reviewed and edited the output and take full responsibility for the content. The same declaration appears in the project database and on the web calculator.")
    d.add("toc")
    d.pb()

