def intro(d):
    d.h1("1. Introduction", bm="sec_intro", pb=True)
    d.h2("1.1 Background")
    d.p("The Biofuels Act of 2006 obliges every litre of diesel sold in the Philippines to contain a minimum share of biodiesel [@ra9367]. In practice the mandate is met with coconut methyl ester. The Act defines biodiesel as fatty acid methyl ester or another mono-alkyl ester derived from vegetable oil or animal fats, technically proven and approved by the Department of Energy, and requires biofuels to meet Philippine National Standards [@ra9367]. HTL produces hydrocarbon renewable diesel and naphtha, not an ester, so cost parity with CME does not by itself make HTL fuel eligible for the mandate; algal FAME would also need DOE approval of its feedstock. Throughout this report CME is used as an economic benchmark, and eligibility is not assumed. The blend has stood at 2–3% for most of the past decade; a scheduled step to 4% in 2025 was suspended [@gma2025], and in August 2026 the government again postponed the move to 5% because CME cost about PHP 135 per litre against PHP 77–97.5 for diesel [@mb2026,cereno2026]. A mandate tied to a single feedstock makes compliance cost move with the coconut oil market.")
    d.p("Microalgae are often proposed as a second feedstock. In principle they need no arable land, grow on saline or waste water, and can take carbon dioxide from flue gas [@chisti2007,quinn2015]. The most detailed public designs are those of the US national laboratories, which have published harmonised farm, conversion and life-cycle models since 2012 and benchmark them each year against measured performance [@davis2012,davis2016,jones2014,klein2024,zhu2023]. These designs report fuel prices of about 4–5 USD per gallon gasoline-equivalent for mature, very large plants, and measured outdoor productivities of about 9–21 g/m²/d, against design targets of 25 [@klein2024,mcgowen2023,huntley2015,jo2020].")
    d.h2("1.2 The study re-assessed here")
    d.p("In 2018 S. G. Cereno and co-authors designed a plant to treat the wastewater of San Carlos BioPower, an 18 MW bagasse-fired power station in San Carlos City, Negros Occidental, and to make biodiesel from *Chlorella vulgaris* grown in 96 open raceway ponds, with hydrothermal liquefaction followed by transesterification [@cereno2018,unfccc2012]. The design came with a full material and energy balance, plant layout and financial model. In 2021 Aviso et al. compared three variants of the plant by net present value, return on investment, payback and jobs, and ranked the variant with wastewater nutrients, HTL and glycerol purification first, with an NPV of PHP 1.04 billion and 219 jobs [@aviso2021]. In a draft working paper prepared for the DIW–CentraleSupélec Winter School (December 2026) we re-analysed the 2021 cash flows on an unlevered basis and found a levelised cost of 0.67–0.74 EUR per litre (0.79–0.87 USD), well below CME [@cereno2026]; this report supersedes that result.")
    d.p("Those results rest on the original material balance, which none of the evaluations recomputed. This report does so, and asks what the plant would cost and emit once every stream is grown, heated, upgraded and supplied as it would have to be in practice. It follows the structure used for an earlier integrated assessment by the same group, in which one balance drives both the TEA and the LCA and every scenario is recalculated in full.")
    d.h2("1.3 Research questions")
    d.bullets(["RQ1. Does the inherited mass and energy balance hold when its key quantities are recomputed from stoichiometry, measured productivity and thermal duties?",
            "RQ2. On one balance driving both models, what are the minimum fuel selling price and the life-cycle greenhouse-gas intensity of corrected HTL and FAME designs at the San Carlos site in 2026?",
            "RQ3. Which design, energy, policy and accounting choices change the answer, where are the break-even and crossover points, and how do the results evolve to 2050?",
            "RQ4. How robust are the results to parametric, correlated and stochastic uncertainty, and which parameters drive the variance?",
            "RQ5. Which designs are Pareto-optimal for cost, climate and capital, today and under future energy pathways?",
            "RQ6. Can a machine-learning surrogate reproduce the engine accurately enough to drive an interactive calculator?"])
    d.h2("1.4 Scope of the analyses")
    d.p("«tab:scope» maps the analyses requested for this study to the sections of the report and to the sheets of the project database. Every figure is also stored in the database with the data behind it.")
    rows = [["Parameter registry", "3", "01_Registry"], ["Process and logistics modelling", "2, 4", "06_Streams_*, 07_Balance, 26_Logistics"], ["Bottom-up techno-economic analysis", "6", "10_TEA_Capital to 12_Cashflow"],
            ["Model verification and validation", "5", "04_Verification, 05_Validation"], ["Life cycle assessment", "7", "08_LCI, 09_LCIA"], ["Scenario analysis (60 scenarios)", "8", "13_Scenarios, 14_Bridge"],
            ["Sensitivity analysis and tornado plots", "9", "15_OAT_Tornado"], ["Break-even and crossover analysis", "10", "16_Breakeven"], ["Prospective analysis to 2050", "11", "17_Prospective"],
            ["Uncertainty analysis and Monte Carlo simulation", "12", "18_MC_Summary, 19_MC_Draws"], ["Correlation and dependency analysis", "13", "21_Correlation"], ["Risk and stochastic uncertainty analysis", "14", "20_Risk, 23_Stochastic"],
            ["Global sensitivity analysis", "15", "22_GSA"], ["Pareto analysis and multi-objective optimisation", "16", "24_Pareto"], ["Machine-learning surrogate modelling", "17", "25_Surrogate"],
            ["Surrogate validation", "18", "25_Surrogate"], ["Prototype web calculator", "19", "README (link)"], ["Research documentation and reporting", "22", "00_README, 27_Figure_Index, F-sheets"]]
    d.table("scope", "Analyses, report sections and database sheets.", ["Analysis", "Section", "Database sheet(s)"], rows, widths=[3.2, 0.9, 2.8])

def system(d):
    d.h1("2. System description", bm="sec_system", pb=True)
    d.h2("2.1 Site and goal")
    d.p(f"The plant sits next to San Carlos BioPower in the San Carlos Ecozone, Negros Occidental, which burns bagasse and cane trash and was registered as a CDM project in 2012 [@unfccc2012]. Its flue gas is the CO₂ source; because the carbon is biogenic and the power plant runs regardless, the flue gas enters the system burden-free, and only the electricity to blow it into the ponds is charged. The goal is to estimate, for a first commercial plant, the cost and climate performance of fuel made from algae grown on that CO₂, and to compare them with CME and fossil diesel. The functional unit is 1 MJ of liquid fuel (lower heating value) at the plant gate; costs are also reported per litre of diesel-equivalent (35.9 MJ/L) so that renewable diesel, naphtha and FAME can be compared with each other and with the CME price of {f2(B['p_cme'])} USD per litre, which equals {f2(CME)} USD per litre diesel-equivalent.")
    d.h2("2.2 Routes and base designs")
    d.p(f"Both routes share the cultivation and harvest section: {f0(H['area_ha'])} ha of unlined raceway ponds with paddlewheels, 20 cm deep, receiving flue-gas CO₂ through sumps, followed by gravity settlers, hollow-fibre membranes and a disc-stack centrifuge that bring the biomass from about 0.05% to 20% solids, as in the NREL farm design [@davis2016,klein2024]. The base area was chosen as a first commercial scale between the original 10.4 ha and the 2,000 ha of the NREL design; Section 8 tests 10.4 to 2,000 ha.")
    d.bullets([f"**Route A, HTL (base).** The 20% slurry is pumped to 20.9 MPa, heated to 350 °C through a feed–effluent heat exchanger and liquefied. A three-phase separator splits biocrude, an aqueous phase rich in nitrogen and organics, gas and solids. The biocrude is hydrotreated with hydrogen to renewable diesel and naphtha [@jones2014,zhu2023]. The aqueous phase is recycled to the ponds as a nutrient source in the base case, or treated by catalytic hydrothermal gasification (CHG) or anaerobic digestion (AD) in scenarios.",
               "**Route B, FAME.** The wet biomass is extracted with hexane, the solvent stripped and recovered, and the triglyceride fraction transesterified with methanol and sodium hydroxide; FAME is washed and glycerol neutralised with phosphoric acid [@davis2012,pradhan2012]. The extracted residue, together with the non-transesterifiable part of the extract (polar lipids and pigments, returned from the lipid clean-up as stream 19), goes to anaerobic digestion, and a biogas CHP unit supplies electricity and heat; digestate nitrogen returns to the ponds [@davis2012,ramos2014]."])
    d.p("The original 2018 design used HTL but then treated the biocrude as if it were triglyceride and transesterified it [@cereno2018]. HTL biocrude is a mixture of fatty acids, nitrogen heterocycles and phenolics with 5–10% nitrogen and oxygen and is upgraded by hydrotreating, not by transesterification [@jones2014,zhu2019]. The FAME route therefore represents the chemistry the original design intended, and the HTL route the chemistry its reactor would actually produce.")
    d.h2("2.3 System boundary and process flow diagrams")
    d.p("«fig:F01a_system_boundary» shows the system boundary. The foreground (green dashed box) contains every unit that would be built and operated at the site; the background supplies electricity, fuel for process heat, hydrogen, fertilisers, chemicals, water treatment, transport and construction materials from ecoinvent 3.12 [@ecoinvent312] or, for electricity, from our Philippine grid model [@evidente2026grid]. The boundary is cradle-to-gate plus the methane and nitrous oxide emitted when the fuel is burnt; biogenic CO₂ taken up by the algae and released at combustion cancels. Products are delivered to the Bacolod depot (included); retail distribution and dispensing beyond the depot are excluded. The 94 g CO₂e/MJ comparator used for GHG savings is the RED II regulatory fossil-fuel comparator, not a measured Philippine diesel inventory [@red2]. «fig:F01b_pfd_htl» and «fig:F01c_pfd_fame» are the process flow diagrams of the two routes, with numbered streams whose annual flows are listed in «tab:streams_htl» and in Appendix C.")
    d.fig("F01a_system_boundary")
    d.fig("F01b_pfd_htl")
    d.fig("F01c_pfd_fame")
    sh = FIG["F01b_pfd_htl"]["data"]
    d.table("streams_htl", "Stream table of Route A at the 2026 base design (annual flows; numbers as in Figure 2).", ["Stream", "Description", "Flow", "Unit", "Note"],
            [[r["stream"], r["description"], (f"{r['value']:,.3g}" if abs(r['value']) < 100 else f"{r['value']:,.0f}"), r["unit"], r.get("note", "")] for r in sh], widths=[0.6, 2.6, 1.0, 1.1, 2.6], align=["l", "l", "r", "l", "l"], size=15)
    d.h2("2.4 Allocation and multifunctionality")
    d.p("HTL yields renewable diesel and naphtha, both fuels, so no allocation between them is needed. The FAME route also yields crude glycerol and, in some designs, surplus electricity. Following the energy-allocation principle of RED II [@red2], burdens are shared by lower heating value with a single factor over the whole system. This does not reproduce the RED II rules for cogeneration, useful heat and allocation at specific process steps, so the savings reported here are indicative and are not a compliance calculation. System expansion, crediting glycerol and exported electricity with the products they displace, is tested in scenarios S57 and S60 [@iso14044].")

NTRI = sum(r["dist"] == "tri" for r in REG.R); NUNI = sum(r["dist"] == "uni" for r in REG.R)
def registry_sec(d):
    d.h1("3. Parameter registry", bm="sec_registry", pb=True)
    d.p(f"All {NPAR} numerical inputs of the engine sit in a single registry (database sheet 01_Registry). Each entry has a base value, a low and a high value, a distribution, a confidence rating, its sources and a note on how it was derived. The registry is the only place where a number enters the model: the TEA, the LCA, the scenarios, the Monte Carlo and the surrogates all read from it. Its nine groups are cultivation; lipid and FAME conversion; HTL and upgrading; energy and emission factors; prices; capital cost basis; finance and policy; logistics; and the stochastic processes used in the cash-flow simulation.")
    d.p("Confidence ratings follow a pedigree-type logic adapted from Weidema and Wesnæs [@weidema1996]: **A** for values measured or designed for the same process in peer-reviewed or national-laboratory design reports; **B** for published values for a closely related process, location or time; **C** for derived or engineering estimates checked against one or more sources; and **D** for assumptions with no direct source, which are flagged for future data collection. «fig:F24_registry_confidence» shows the ratings by group.")
    d.fig("F24_registry_confidence")
    d.p(f"Of the {NPAR} parameters, {CONF['A']} are rated A, {CONF['B']} B, {CONF['C']} C and {CONF['D']} D. The D-rated parameters are listed in «tab:dpar». Most are scenario options that do not enter the base design. Six enter the HTL base case. Three matter most: the land lease, the frequency of damaging typhoons and the production days lost per typhoon; the other three (repair cost per typhoon, length of the flue-gas line and the sea distance for imported chemicals) have small effects. None of the six is among the 30 parameters retained for the Sobol' analysis (on the combined cost-and-climate Morris score the first three rank {mrank('p_land')}, {mrank('lam_typh')} and {mrank('typh_days')} of {NMOR} for HTL; on cost alone the land lease and the typhoon rate rank {mrank('p_land', True)}th and {mrank('lam_typh', True)}th), but they matter to the risk analysis and should be replaced with site data.")
    d.table("dpar", "Parameters with confidence rating D (assumption without direct source).", ["Key", "Parameter", "Base", "Unit", "Basis"],
            [[r["key"], r["name"], f"{r['base']:g}", r["unit"], r["note"]] for r in DPAR], widths=[1.0, 2.3, 0.7, 1.0, 3.2], size=15)
    d.p(f"Ranges are triangular for {NTRI} parameters and uniform for {NUNI}. They were set from the spread of published values, not from a fixed percentage. Productivity, for example, ranges from 10 to 30 g/m²/d around a base of 20: the lower bound is close to the 8.6–9.9 g/m²/d averaged in 675 m² raceways in Korea [@jo2020]; the base equals the long-run assumption of Lundquist et al. (20), is close to the best case of a hybrid photobioreactor–pond system in Hawaii (20.5), and lies above the 16.7 g/m²/d measured by NREL in 2023 on an Arizona testbed with selected strains, which is a benchmark rather than a measurement for *Chlorella* at San Carlos [@huntley2015,lundquist2010,klein2024]; the upper bound is above the 25 g/m²/d design target of the US programme [@davis2016]. The most influential entries are reproduced with their sources in «tab:keypar»; the complete registry is in Appendix B and in the database.")
    keys = ["prod", "avail", "harv_rec", "co2_util", "mix_kwh", "dew_kwh", "co2_kwh", "Nfrac", "lipid", "fame_frac", "htl_y", "htl_hr", "ht_h2", "ht_y", "ef_grid", "p_el", "p_h2", "p_urea", "cap_farm_acre", "cap_htl", "loc_f", "r", "life", "tax"]
    rows = []
    for k in keys:
        r = REG.P[k]; rows.append([r["name"], f"{r['base']:g}", f"{r['low']:g}–{r['high']:g}", r["unit"], r["conf"], [{"citek": r["src"]}] if r["src"] else "derived"])
    d.b.append({"k": "table", "tid": "keypar", "bm": "tab_keypar", "cap": [ "Key parameters of the registry (full list in Appendix B)."], "head": [[h] for h in ["Parameter", "Base", "Range", "Unit", "Conf.", "Sources"]],
                "rows": [[(c if isinstance(c, list) else [str(c)]) for c in row] for row in rows], "widths": [2.6, 0.8, 1.1, 1.3, 0.5, 1.4], "align": ["l", "r", "r", "l", "l", "l"], "note": None, "size": 15})

def process(d):
    d.h1("4. Process and logistics modelling", bm="sec_process", pb=True)
    d.p("The engine is a steady-state annual balance written in Python. It takes the registry and a design vector (route, pond area, liner, CO₂ source, nutrient source, aqueous-phase treatment, upgrading location, heat source, electricity supply, allocation) and returns every stream, utility, inventory flow, capital item, cost, cash flow and impact. One evaluation takes a few milliseconds in Python, which is what makes the 60 scenarios, the Monte Carlo and Sobol' runs and the optimisation possible on the full model. The main equations are given below; Appendix E lists all 33 equations of the engine and the analyses.")
    d.h2("4.1 Cultivation and harvest")
    d.p("Biomass grown per year follows from productivity, pond area, the on-stream factor and the expected loss to typhoons; P is the mean productivity on operating days, so downtime is counted once, through a:")
    d.eq("m_grown = 0.01 × P × A × 365 × a × (1 − λ·d/365)   [t/yr; P in g/m²/d, A in ha]", 1)
    d.p(f"with P the annual average productivity (g AFDW/m²/d), A the pond area, a the on-stream factor ({B['avail']}) that covers culture crashes and maintenance [@davis2016,mcgowen2023], λ the rate of damaging typhoons ({B['lam_typh']} per year) and d the production days lost per event ({B['typh_days']:g}) [@corporal2017,cinco2016]. Harvested biomass is m_grown times the harvest recovery ({B['harv_rec']}). CO₂ delivered is set by the stoichiometric uptake of {B['co2_fix']} kg CO₂ per kg AFDW, which corresponds to 49.9% carbon and is within 3% of the elemental formula of Chisti [@chisti2007], divided by the fraction of delivered CO₂ that the culture takes up ({B['co2_util']}) [@degodos2014,eustance2020,klein2023]:")
    d.eq("m_CO₂ = 1.83 · m_grown / u_CO₂ ;   m_N = N_frac · m_grown / η_nut", 2)
    d.p(f"Nitrogen and phosphorus demands use a biomass composition of {B['Nfrac']} kg N and {B['Pfrac']} kg P per kg AFDW and an uptake efficiency of {B['nut_eff']} [@chisti2007,davis2012]. Phosphorus is bought as diammonium phosphate; nitrogen is the remainder after DAP nitrogen and any recycled nitrogen and is bought as urea. Electricity for mixing ({B['mix_kwh']:g} kWh/ha/d), CO₂ delivery ({B['co2_kwh']:g} kWh/t CO₂), dewatering ({B['dew_kwh']:g} kWh/t) and water circulation ({B['circ_kwh']:g} kWh/t) follows the NREL design and the measurements of Zheng et al. [@davis2016,clippinger2019,zheng2016]. Make-up water is evaporation minus rainfall, {B['makeup_mm']} mm/d, which is low because Negros receives about 3.6 mm/d of rain [@pagasa_dgte].")
    d.h2("4.2 Conversion")
    d.p("For Route A the HTL heat duty is the sensible heat to bring the slurry from 30 to 350 °C, less what the feed–effluent exchanger recovers:")
    d.eq("Q_HTL = (m_dry / x_s) · c_p · ΔT · (1 − ε)", 3)
    d.p(f"with x_s = {B['htl_solids']} the slurry solids, c_p = 3.8 kJ/kg/K, ΔT = {B['htl_dT']:g} K and ε = {B['htl_hr']} [@jones2014,anastasakis2018,ong2018]. Biocrude is the harvested AFDW times the HTL yield ({B['htl_y']}, from the PNNL Chlorella run at 348 °C, with 0.33 for the 2022 state of technology and 0.59 for the design case [@jones2014,zhu2023]). Hydrotreating consumes {B['ht_h2']} kg H₂ per kg biocrude and yields {B['ht_y']} kg fuel per kg, of which {pct(B['diesel_frac'])} is diesel-range [@jones2014,zhu2023]. Hydrogen comes from a small LPG-fed reformer in the TEA; the LCA represents it by the ecoinvent market for low-pressure hydrogen with its GWP set to {B['ef_h2']} kg CO₂e/kg, a proxy rather than a process-matched inventory, whose effect is tested in the sensitivity analysis (range {REG.P['ef_h2']['low']:g}–{REG.P['ef_h2']['high']:g}) and in S32 (low-carbon hydrogen). In the aqueous phase {pct(B['aq_Nfrac'])} of the feed nitrogen is recovered, and {pct(B['aq_Nrec'])} of it is taken up again when recycled [@zhu2019]. Recycled nitrogen is computed on the harvested biomass that actually enters HTL ({f0(H['N_rec'])} t N/yr); nitrogen in the 5% not harvested is not credited.")
    d.p(f"For Route B, extracted lipid is AFDW × lipid content ({B['lipid']}) × extraction recovery ({B['ext_eff']}), and FAME is that lipid times the transesterifiable fraction ({B['fame_frac']}) and the conversion ({B['tx_conv']}) [@davis2012,laurens2012,freedman1984]. The transesterifiable fraction matters most: for *C. vulgaris* it ranges from 31% of the extract under nitrogen-replete growth to 93% under nitrogen starvation [@laurens2012]; wastewater-grown biomass is nutrient-replete. The lipid-extracted residue and the returned non-transesterifiable extract ({f0(F['extract_ret'])} t/yr; together {f0(F['ad_feed'])} t/yr of volatile solids) are digested at {B['ad_ch4']} m³ CH₄ per kg volatile solids, a conservative yield for the lipid-rich extract, with {pct(B['ch4_leak'])} methane slip, and burnt in a CHP unit with {pct(B['chp_el'])} electrical and {pct(B['chp_th'])} thermal efficiency [@ramos2014,bohutskyi2015,frank2013].")
    d.h2("4.3 Mass and energy balances")
    d.p(f"«fig:F06_mass_balance» and «fig:F05_energy_balance» show the annual balances of the base designs. The HTL plant grows {f0(H['grown'])} t AFDW per year, harvests {f0(H['afdw'])} t and makes {f0(H['biocrude'])} t of biocrude and {f0(H['prod']['rd'])} t of renewable diesel plus {f0(H['prod']['naph'])} t of naphtha, or {f2(H['ldeq']/1e6)} million litres diesel-equivalent. It uses {f1(H['el_dem']/1e6)} GWh of electricity, {f1(H['heat_net']/1e6)} TJ of process heat and {f0(H['h2'])} t of hydrogen. The FAME plant makes {f0(F['prod']['fame'])} t of FAME ({f2(F['ldeq']/1e6)} million litres deq) and {f0(F['prod']['glyc_crude'])} t of crude glycerol, and exports {f1(F['el_export']/1e6)} GWh of electricity from biogas. The HTL route turns {pct(H['fuel_mj']/(H['afdw']*1000*22.0))} of the biomass energy into fuel and the FAME route {pct(F['fuel_mj']/(F['afdw']*1000*22.0))}, which is the root of the factor-of-three difference in their costs.")
    d.fig("F06_mass_balance"); d.fig("F05_energy_balance")
    d.h2("4.4 Logistics")
    d.p(f"Fuel, chemicals and fertilisers move by road and sea. The base design sends renewable diesel 80 km by road to a Bacolod depot, with half of the product moving on average a further 300 km by coastal tanker, and imports chemicals and fertilisers by sea (whose freight is part of their delivered price and is counted in the inventory); alternatives include the San Carlos–Toledo roll-on/roll-off ferry to Cebu, coastal tankers to Batangas and Manila, and shipping the biocrude to the Petron refinery in Limay, Bataan, for co-processing [@pamasahe2026,jica2005,llanto2010]. Road freight costs {B['c_road']} USD per t-km and coastal shipping {B['c_sea']} [@suarez2022,jica2005]; emissions use the ecoinvent lorry and tanker datasets. «fig:F23_logistics» and «tab:logistics» compare the options.")
    rows = [[l["config"], f0(l["cost"] / 1e3), f2(l["mfsp"]), f2(l["gwp_log"]), f1(l["gwp"])] for l in LOG]
    d.table("logistics", "Logistics options for Route A (2026 base). Cost and GWP of the logistics chain; MFSP and total GWP of the plant.", ["Configuration", "Logistics cost, kUSD/yr", "MFSP, USD/L deq", "Logistics GWP, g/MJ", "Total GWP, g/MJ"], rows, widths=[3.2, 1.1, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0], align=["l", "r", "r", "r", "r"])
    d.fig("F23_logistics")
    d.p(f"Logistics are small: under 2% of production cost and at most {max(l['gwp_log'] for l in LOG):.1f} g CO₂e/MJ in every option. The one option that changes the result is co-processing at the refinery, which avoids the on-site hydrotreater and lowers the MFSP to {f2(S['S07']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq, but its tolling fee is a D-rated assumption.")
